= Autopoiesis — Simple =

'''Autopoiesis''' is a theory about how living systems maintain themselves as organized unities. The word means roughly “self-production.”

Humberto Maturana and Francisco Varela developed the concept in the 1970s. Their central idea is that a living system is not defined only by a list of parts. It is defined by a network of processes that continually produces the parts and boundary that allow that same network to continue operating.<ref name="vmu">[https://doi.org/10.1016/0303-2647(74)90031-8 Varela, F. G., Maturana, H. R. and Uribe, R. (1974), “Autopoiesis: The organization of living systems, its characterization and a model.”]</ref>

== A cell as the main example ==

A living cell continually builds and replaces many of its components.

Its chemical reactions produce proteins, lipids, and other molecules. Some of those molecules help maintain the cell membrane. The membrane helps create the conditions under which the internal reactions can continue.

A simplified loop is:

<pre>
reactions
→ components
→ membrane and internal machinery
→ conditions for reactions
</pre>

The important point is the loop: the organization produces components that help regenerate the organization.

== The cell is not isolated ==

Autopoiesis does '''not''' mean that an organism makes everything from nothing.

A cell needs matter and energy from its environment. It takes in nutrients, exchanges gases and ions, releases waste, and loses heat.

So an autopoietic system can be physically open while being organizationally self-producing.

== Boundary matters ==

The boundary is part of the theory.

A membrane separates the system from its environment and controls some of the exchange between them.

If a laboratory reaction is placed inside a container that an experimenter must permanently provide and repair, the container alone does not show that the reaction system produces its own boundary.

Researchers therefore ask whether the system itself makes and maintains the components that establish its unity.

== Not the same as self-organization ==

[[Self-organization]] is a broader idea.

A crystal, vortex, or chemical pattern can form organized structure from local interactions.

That does not necessarily make it autopoietic.

Autopoiesis requires a recursive production network that constitutes and maintains the system itself.

== Not the same as homeostasis ==

[[Homeostasis]] means regulating variables such as temperature, pH, or chemical concentration.

A thermostat is homeostatic in a simple sense because it regulates temperature.

It is not autopoietic because it does not produce the components that make the thermostat.

Living systems can use both homeostasis and autopoiesis.

== Not the same as reproduction ==

Maturana and Varela separated autopoiesis from reproduction.

An individual organism can remain alive even if it is sterile.

Reproduction is important for evolution, but the organization of an individual living system must already exist before it can reproduce.

== Why the concept matters ==

Autopoiesis shifted attention from “What parts do living things contain?” toward “How are the processes of a living system organized so that the system continually constitutes itself?”

That question has influenced:

* theoretical biology;
* origin-of-life research;
* [[Artificial life]];
* systems theory;
* philosophy of biology;
* theories of cognition.

== Testing the idea ==

For a real chemical or biological system, researchers can ask:

* Does it make its own boundary components?
* Does it regenerate catalysts and machinery needed for production?
* Can it maintain organization while components turn over?
* Can it recover from small disturbances?
* Which functions must still be supplied from outside?

These questions convert the theory into measurable mechanisms.

== Artificial systems ==

A computer simulation can model an autopoietic network.

That does not automatically mean the physical computer is autopoietic or that the simulated entity is biologically alive.

Likewise, an AI system that edits its own code is not automatically autopoietic. The relevant issue is whether it actually produces and maintains the components and constraints that constitute its own organization.

== Limits ==

Autopoiesis is influential, but it is not the only definition of life.

Other definitions emphasize metabolism, heredity, reproduction, evolution, adaptation, or combinations of these.

Extensions of autopoiesis to societies or software may be useful, but they need explicit mechanisms. A metaphorical resemblance to self-maintenance is not enough to establish biological autopoiesis.

